1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,272
You've built a golden set, a calibrated rubric, and a validated judge.

2
00:00:04,272 --> 00:00:08,867
Here's the question this lecture is actually about: what do you do with the number

3
00:00:08,867 --> 00:00:11,109
once you have it, release after release.

4
00:00:11,109 --> 00:00:12,902
Most teams do the obvious thing.

5
00:00:12,902 --> 00:00:17,104
They watch one aggregate pass rate, and if it doesn't move much, they ship.

6
00:00:17,104 --> 00:00:17,945
Sound familiar?

7
00:00:17,945 --> 00:00:22,315
That habit is about to cost you, and I want to show you exactly how, before it

8
00:00:22,315 --> 00:00:25,341
happens to your feature instead of a hypothetical one.

9
00:00:25,341 --> 00:00:28,367
A ninety-two percent pass rate is not one measurement.

10
00:00:28,367 --> 00:00:33,018
It's fifty measurements averaged into a single number, and averaging is exactly the

11
00:00:33,018 --> 00:00:36,716
operation that hides a problem living in a small part of your set.

12
00:00:36,716 --> 00:00:41,143
This is the same instinct from earlier in this section, applied one step later.

13
00:00:41,143 --> 00:00:45,345
You built four buckets so a random fifty couldn't hide a rare failure mode.

14
00:00:45,345 --> 00:00:49,772
Track only the blended number afterwards, and you've thrown that structure away

15
00:00:49,772 --> 00:00:52,013
right when it started paying for itself.

16
00:00:52,563 --> 00:00:54,326
Here's a real shape of change.

17
00:00:54,326 --> 00:00:58,085
A prompt update went out, meant to make responses more thorough.

18
00:00:58,085 --> 00:01:01,904
Watch what it did, bucket by bucket, not just at the bottom line.

19
00:01:01,904 --> 00:01:05,487
Common path improved, nineteen of twenty instead of eighteen.

20
00:01:05,487 --> 00:01:06,956
Known failure modes held.

21
00:01:06,956 --> 00:01:07,896
Edge cases held.

22
00:01:07,896 --> 00:01:09,129
Nothing alarming yet.

23
00:01:09,129 --> 00:01:13,535
Then adversarial: five out of five, before, down to two out of five, after.

24
00:01:13,535 --> 00:01:18,293
The instruction to be "more thorough and complete" made the model measurably more

25
00:01:18,293 --> 00:01:22,523
willing to comply with a cleverly worded request it should have refused.

26
00:01:22,523 --> 00:01:24,285
Now look at the aggregate row.

27
00:01:24,285 --> 00:01:26,635
Ninety-two percent down to eighty-eight.

28
00:01:26,635 --> 00:01:28,574
Four points, on a fifty-case set.

29
00:01:28,574 --> 00:01:31,041
Most teams would call that noise and ship.

30
00:01:31,041 --> 00:01:35,447
The bucket breakdown says otherwise: one specific capability didn't dip, it

31
00:01:35,447 --> 00:01:40,146
collapsed, and the aggregate buried it under thirty-nine cases that got slightly

32
00:01:40,146 --> 00:01:40,557
better.

33
00:01:41,107 --> 00:01:43,376
Notice what the definition rules out.

34
00:01:43,376 --> 00:01:48,465
Not "one case flipped": a single golden-set case is one data point, and data points

35
00:01:48,465 --> 00:01:48,895
wobble.

36
00:01:48,895 --> 00:01:53,984
Not "the aggregate moved a bit": you just watched four points of aggregate movement

37
00:01:53,984 --> 00:01:55,885
mean almost nothing on its own.

38
00:01:55,885 --> 00:02:00,606
What counts is a specific bucket moving more than ordinary re-run noise would

39
00:02:00,606 --> 00:02:01,097
explain.

40
00:02:01,097 --> 00:02:06,125
Five out of five to two out of five, in a bucket that exists specifically to catch

41
00:02:06,125 --> 00:02:08,271
this kind of failure, is not noise.

42
00:02:08,271 --> 00:02:10,111
It's the bucket doing its job.

43
00:02:10,111 --> 00:02:14,096
The honest version of this rule is exactly that, a rule of thumb.

44
00:02:14,096 --> 00:02:19,186
The properly worked-out version, with an actual threshold instead of a gut feeling,

45
00:02:19,186 --> 00:02:21,148
is coming later in this section.

46
00:02:21,148 --> 00:02:25,624
For now, treat any small bucket's sharp drop as a stop sign, not a shrug.

47
00:02:26,174 --> 00:02:29,238
Here's where all of this actually sits in your week.

48
00:02:29,238 --> 00:02:33,892
Someone proposes a change: a new prompt, a different model, a tweaked retrieval

49
00:02:33,892 --> 00:02:34,775
step, anything.

50
00:02:34,775 --> 00:02:39,724
Before it ships, the golden set runs again, all four buckets, not just a spot check.

51
00:02:39,724 --> 00:02:44,673
Then the only question that matters: did any bucket move more than you'd expect from

52
00:02:44,673 --> 00:02:45,027
noise.

53
00:02:45,027 --> 00:02:49,740
If yes, hold, and go read the specific cases in that bucket before anyone argues

54
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:51,213
about it in the abstract.

55
00:02:51,213 --> 00:02:55,572
If no, ship it, and log the new pass rate, by bucket, next to the old one.

56
00:02:55,572 --> 00:03:00,462
That log is the part most teams skip, and it's the part that turns "feels about the

57
00:03:00,462 --> 00:03:02,583
same" into a chart you can point at.

58
00:03:02,583 --> 00:03:04,822
None of this requires new tooling yet.

59
00:03:04,822 --> 00:03:09,712
A shared spreadsheet, one row per release, four bucket columns and a date, does the

60
00:03:09,712 --> 00:03:12,952
job until you have a reason to build something fancier.

61
00:03:13,502 --> 00:03:18,413
Remember the promise from earlier in this section: online signal gets a proper home,

62
00:03:18,413 --> 00:03:22,271
a dashboard, instead of a Slack message when a customer complains.

63
00:03:22,271 --> 00:03:26,364
Here's what that home actually needs, and it's less than people build.

64
00:03:26,364 --> 00:03:31,041
Pass rate broken out by bucket, always, never one blended figure standing in for

65
00:03:31,041 --> 00:03:32,502
four different questions.

66
00:03:32,502 --> 00:03:37,121
Every version plotted against the last, so a trend is visible, not just today's

67
00:03:37,121 --> 00:03:37,647
snapshot.

68
00:03:37,647 --> 00:03:42,558
And your online samples, pulled from live traffic every release, sitting on the same

69
00:03:42,558 --> 00:03:45,540
chart as the offline number they're meant to check.

70
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:47,527
Watch for the kink, not the level.

71
00:03:47,527 --> 00:03:50,509
A bucket sitting at ninety percent forever is fine.

72
00:03:50,509 --> 00:03:55,127
A bucket that bends sharply in one release is the thing that was actually worth

73
00:03:55,127 --> 00:03:56,180
building this for.

74
00:03:56,180 --> 00:04:01,091
Here's what that bend actually looks like once you plot it instead of describing it.

75
00:04:01,641 --> 00:04:03,307
Two points isn't a trend yet.

76
00:04:03,307 --> 00:04:04,456
It's barely a slope.

77
00:04:04,456 --> 00:04:09,109
That's the honest state of this particular chart today, and it's also exactly why

78
00:04:09,109 --> 00:04:13,705
you keep it running instead of screenshotting one before-and-after: it gets more

79
00:04:13,705 --> 00:04:15,486
useful every release, not less.

80
00:04:15,486 --> 00:04:20,254
Look at where four of the five lines sit at the second point: bunched near the top,

81
00:04:20,254 --> 00:04:21,001
barely moved.

82
00:04:21,001 --> 00:04:23,586
One line does something the other four don't.

83
00:04:23,586 --> 00:04:24,448
It doesn't dip.

84
00:04:24,448 --> 00:04:29,101
It falls through the floor of the chart, on its own, apart from everything around

85
00:04:29,101 --> 00:04:29,274
it.

86
00:04:29,274 --> 00:04:33,985
That's what a real regression looks like on a line instead of in a row of a table:

87
00:04:33,985 --> 00:04:38,121
not a number that changed, a line that bent while its neighbours didn't.

88
00:04:38,121 --> 00:04:42,659
By the time this chart has ten releases on it, you won't need anyone to explain

89
00:04:42,659 --> 00:04:44,325
which bucket needs attention.

90
00:04:44,325 --> 00:04:46,968
You'll be able to see it from across the room.

91
00:04:47,518 --> 00:04:48,779
Say the cost plainly.

92
00:04:48,779 --> 00:04:51,180
Building the dashboard is the easy part.

93
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:56,162
The ongoing cost is smaller than the calibration work from earlier lectures, but it

94
00:04:56,162 --> 00:05:00,964
never ends: every proposed change means a re-run, and every re-run means someone

95
00:05:00,964 --> 00:05:05,766
actually opens the breakdown and looks, not just glances at whether the top-line

96
00:05:05,766 --> 00:05:06,726
number is green.

97
00:05:06,726 --> 00:05:11,648
Skip that ownership and you get the worst outcome in this course: a dashboard that

98
00:05:11,648 --> 00:05:16,570
exists, looks rigorous in a slide deck, and hasn't been checked in three releases.

99
00:05:16,570 --> 00:05:19,751
That's not a smaller risk than having no eval at all.

100
00:05:19,751 --> 00:05:24,733
In some ways it's a bigger one, because everyone in the room now assumes somebody's

101
00:05:24,733 --> 00:05:29,595
watching, and the launch conversation moves faster precisely because nobody feels

102
00:05:29,595 --> 00:05:31,096
the need to double-check.

103
00:05:31,646 --> 00:05:33,408
One thing before you move on.

104
00:05:33,408 --> 00:05:38,330
Take whatever pass rate you're currently tracking and split it by bucket, even if

105
00:05:38,330 --> 00:05:43,374
that means an afternoon with a spreadsheet and no automation yet, and see whether a

106
00:05:43,374 --> 00:05:45,865
blended number has been hiding something.

107
00:05:45,865 --> 00:05:50,969
Then name a name, not a team, a person, who actually opens this after every release.

108
00:05:50,969 --> 00:05:55,587
A dashboard with no owner quietly reverts to a Slack message within a month.

109
00:05:55,587 --> 00:06:00,509
Next two lectures, we build the thing itself: the eval harness that produces this

110
00:06:00,509 --> 00:06:05,492
breakdown automatically, first in a spreadsheet you can build this afternoon, then

111
00:06:05,492 --> 00:06:08,227
in thirty lines of code that runs it for you.
